## Summary

I shipped a production-polished, Apple-idiomatic **`AICrossRuleConflictDetection`** SwiftUI surface at parity with `web/src/components/ai/AICrossRuleConflictDetection.tsx`, following the established sibling-surface (0006) architecture.

**12 files** in `apps/apple/TeslaSync/shared-surfaces/` (all ≤400 lines):
- `.swift` (main view: gate switch, ready card, freshness chip/banner, a11y summary)
- `.Adapter` / `.Logic` (pure, tested: `tool_result`→`[RuleConflict]` decode mirroring the web `handleEvent` walk, button logic, request body, kind/flag mapping, accessibility)
- `.Model` / `.Source` (P1/S8 state-holder, telemetry, i18n facade, in-memory test source)
- `.Views` / `.Chrome` (header, Helix badge, action button, conflict rows with two "Review rule" buttons + mismatch chips in a wrapping flow layout, output panel, empty/gate chrome)
- `.Previews` (every state), `.strings` (i18n catalog), `.Tests` / `.AdapterTests` / `.ModelTests`

**Parity:** all web states (idle/streaming/empty/conflicts/stream-error) + P4 leaf states (gateLoading/gateError/stale/offline/gatedOff); binds via the shared state-holder (no HTTP in the view); every string through the P1/S10 facade; full VoiceOver + reduce-motion; `view.opened` telemetry.

